1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,005
Welcome to School Physics, Explained.

2
00:00:02,455 --> 00:00:07,442
This is High School Physics, Unit 2,
Vectors and 2D Motion. In this video,

3
00:00:07,442 --> 00:00:10,946
we'll cover five topics, each with a
worked example.

4
00:00:11,396 --> 00:00:13,039
Adding Vectors with Diagrams.

5
00:00:13,489 --> 00:00:15,729
Vector Components with Sine and Cosine.

6
00:00:16,179 --> 00:00:17,480
Relative Velocity.

7
00:00:17,930 --> 00:00:19,807
Projectiles Launched Horizontally.

8
00:00:20,257 --> 00:00:22,049
Projectiles Launched at an Angle.

9
00:00:22,499 --> 00:00:26,254
And at the end, one bigger problem that
puts the whole unit together.

10
00:00:26,704 --> 00:00:27,792
Let's get started.

11
00:00:29,433 --> 00:00:33,941
Walk three blocks east, then four blocks
north. How far are you from where you

12
00:00:33,941 --> 00:00:34,404
started?

13
00:00:34,854 --> 00:00:37,947
Not seven blocks. To find out, we need to
add vectors.

14
00:00:39,097 --> 00:00:42,809
A vector has a size and a direction, and
we draw it as an arrow.

15
00:00:43,259 --> 00:00:47,526
The length of the arrow shows the size.
The way it points shows the direction.

16
00:00:47,976 --> 00:00:52,904
You can slide an arrow anywhere on the
page. As long as its length and direction

17
00:00:52,904 --> 00:00:55,122
stay the same, it's the same vector.

18
00:00:56,272 --> 00:00:59,110
To add two vectors, use the tip to tail
method.

19
00:00:59,560 --> 00:01:03,400
Draw the first arrow. Then start the
second arrow at the tip of the first.

20
00:01:03,850 --> 00:01:08,472
The sum, called the resultant, goes from
the tail of the first arrow to the tip of

21
00:01:08,472 --> 00:01:09,204
the last one.

22
00:01:10,354 --> 00:01:13,725
Back to the walk. Three blocks east, then
four blocks north.

23
00:01:14,175 --> 00:01:18,782
These make a right triangle, so the
Pythagorean theorem gives the size of the

24
00:01:18,782 --> 00:01:19,380
resultant.

25
00:01:19,830 --> 00:01:23,478
The square root of three squared plus four
squared is five blocks.

26
00:01:24,628 --> 00:01:25,866
The order doesn't matter.

27
00:01:26,316 --> 00:01:31,407
Go north first and then east, and you end
up at the same spot. Vector addition is

28
00:01:31,407 --> 00:01:32,161
commutative.

29
00:01:33,311 --> 00:01:35,871
What if the vectors point in opposite
directions?

30
00:01:36,321 --> 00:01:38,902
Walk five meters east, then two meters
west.

31
00:01:39,352 --> 00:01:42,659
The arrows overlap, and the resultant is
three meters east.

32
00:01:43,809 --> 00:01:45,174
Now try one yourself.

33
00:01:45,624 --> 00:01:50,803
A plane flies six kilometers south, then
eight kilometers west. How far is it from

34
00:01:50,803 --> 00:01:52,003
its starting point?

35
00:01:52,453 --> 00:01:53,648
Pause the video now.

36
00:01:54,098 --> 00:01:57,980
The square root of six squared plus eight
squared is ten kilometers.

37
00:01:58,280 --> 00:02:01,437
Now let's work through a complete example,
step by step.

38
00:02:02,587 --> 00:02:07,943
A hiker walks three kilometers east, then
four kilometers north, then six kilometers

39
00:02:07,943 --> 00:02:08,262
west.

40
00:02:08,712 --> 00:02:13,213
Find the hiker's displacement from the
start: how far, and in what direction.

41
00:02:14,363 --> 00:02:18,203
Add the east-west parts together, and the
north-south parts together.

42
00:02:18,653 --> 00:02:23,419
Then combine them with the Pythagorean
theorem, and find the angle with inverse

43
00:02:23,419 --> 00:02:23,901
tangent.

44
00:02:25,051 --> 00:02:29,856
Take east as positive. East-west: plus
three, minus six, gives minus three

45
00:02:29,856 --> 00:02:32,518
kilometers. That's three kilometers west.

46
00:02:32,968 --> 00:02:35,080
North-south: just plus four kilometers.

47
00:02:35,530 --> 00:02:39,753
The size: the square root of three squared
plus four squared, which is five

48
00:02:39,753 --> 00:02:40,373
kilometers.

49
00:02:40,823 --> 00:02:46,045
The angle: inverse tangent of four over
three is about fifty three degrees, north

50
00:02:46,045 --> 00:02:46,561
of west.

51
00:02:47,711 --> 00:02:52,789
The hiker ends up five kilometers from the
start, fifty three degrees north of west.

52
00:02:53,239 --> 00:02:57,925
Compare that with the distance walked:
three plus four plus six is thirteen

53
00:02:57,925 --> 00:03:02,049
kilometers. Distance is the path.
Displacement is start to finish.

54
00:03:02,499 --> 00:03:07,342
Tip: pick a positive direction first, and
give opposite directions a minus sign.

55
00:03:07,792 --> 00:03:11,504
Tip: say which way the angle is measured
from, like north of west.

56
00:03:12,654 --> 00:03:17,897
Your turn. A boat sails five kilometers
north, then twelve kilometers east. Find

57
00:03:17,897 --> 00:03:19,011
its displacement.

58
00:03:19,461 --> 00:03:20,656
Pause the video now.

59
00:03:21,106 --> 00:03:26,193
The square root of five squared plus
twelve squared is thirteen kilometers. The

60
00:03:26,193 --> 00:03:31,215
angle is inverse tangent of five over
twelve, about twenty three degrees north

61
00:03:31,215 --> 00:03:31,730
of east.

62
00:03:32,030 --> 00:03:34,611
You pull a sled with a rope that's tilted
upward.

63
00:03:35,061 --> 00:03:39,923
Part of your pull moves the sled forward,
and part of it lifts. How much goes each

64
00:03:39,923 --> 00:03:40,160
way?

65
00:03:41,310 --> 00:03:45,769
Every vector can be split into two parts
at right angles, called components.

66
00:03:46,219 --> 00:03:50,827
One points along x, the horizontal. The
other points along y, the vertical.

67
00:03:51,277 --> 00:03:54,967
Put them tip to tail, and they add back up
to the original vector.

68
00:03:56,117 --> 00:03:58,997
The vector and its components make a right
triangle.

69
00:03:59,447 --> 00:04:04,354
The vector is the hypotenuse. The angle
theta is measured from the horizontal.

70
00:04:04,804 --> 00:04:10,945
Remember SOH CAH TOA. Cosine is adjacent
over hypotenuse, and sine is opposite over

71
00:04:10,945 --> 00:04:11,759
hypotenuse.

72
00:04:12,909 --> 00:04:17,197
So the horizontal component is the
magnitude times cosine theta.

73
00:04:17,647 --> 00:04:21,316
And the vertical component is the
magnitude times sine theta.

74
00:04:22,466 --> 00:04:26,749
Back to the sled. You pull with fifty
newtons, at forty degrees above the

75
00:04:26,749 --> 00:04:27,394
horizontal.

76
00:04:27,844 --> 00:04:32,601
The forward part is fifty times cosine
forty, about thirty eight newtons.

77
00:04:33,051 --> 00:04:37,446
The upward part is fifty times sine forty,
about thirty two newtons.

78
00:04:38,596 --> 00:04:39,620
Watch the signs.

79
00:04:40,070 --> 00:04:42,886
A component pointing left, or down, is
negative.

80
00:04:43,336 --> 00:04:48,805
So a vector pointing down and to the left
has a negative x component, and a negative

81
00:04:48,805 --> 00:04:49,587
y component.

82
00:04:50,737 --> 00:04:52,102
Now try one yourself.

83
00:04:52,552 --> 00:04:57,224
A ball is kicked at twelve meters per
second, at thirty degrees above the

84
00:04:57,224 --> 00:05:01,576
ground. Find the horizontal and vertical
components of its velocity.

85
00:05:02,026 --> 00:05:03,221
Pause the video now.

86
00:05:03,671 --> 00:05:08,722
The horizontal part is twelve cosine
thirty, about ten point four meters per

87
00:05:08,722 --> 00:05:14,039
second. The vertical part is twelve sine
thirty, which is six meters per second.

88
00:05:14,339 --> 00:05:17,496
Now let's work through a complete example,
step by step.

89
00:05:18,646 --> 00:05:22,593
A drone flies fifty meters at thirty seven
degrees north of east.

90
00:05:23,043 --> 00:05:25,198
Then it flies twenty meters straight
north.

91
00:05:25,648 --> 00:05:31,024
Find its total displacement. Use sine
thirty seven equals zero point six, and

92
00:05:31,024 --> 00:05:34,096
cosine thirty seven equals zero point
eight.

93
00:05:35,246 --> 00:05:39,214
The vectors aren't at right angles, so
split each one into components.

94
00:05:39,664 --> 00:05:42,565
Add the x parts, add the y parts, then
combine.

95
00:05:43,715 --> 00:05:49,318
Components of A: fifty times zero point
eight is forty meters east. Fifty times

96
00:05:49,318 --> 00:05:52,014
zero point six is thirty meters north.

97
00:05:52,464 --> 00:05:55,066
Components of B: zero east, twenty north.

98
00:05:55,516 --> 00:05:58,546
Add them: forty meters east, and fifty
meters north.

99
00:05:58,996 --> 00:06:03,607
Size: the square root of forty squared
plus fifty squared, which is about sixty

100
00:06:03,607 --> 00:06:04,308
four meters.

101
00:06:04,758 --> 00:06:09,762
Angle: inverse tangent of fifty over
forty, about fifty one degrees north of

102
00:06:09,762 --> 00:06:10,091
east.

103
00:06:11,241 --> 00:06:16,318
The drone ends up about sixty four meters
away, fifty one degrees north of east.

104
00:06:16,768 --> 00:06:21,184
Tip: cosine goes with the side next to the
angle. Here that's the east side.

105
00:06:21,634 --> 00:06:27,049
Tip: you can't just add fifty and twenty.
Magnitudes only add when vectors point the

106
00:06:27,049 --> 00:06:27,629
same way.

107
00:06:28,079 --> 00:06:33,055
Tip: check the answer makes sense. Sixty
four is less than seventy, as it should

108
00:06:33,055 --> 00:06:33,242
be.

109
00:06:34,392 --> 00:06:39,461
Your turn. You pull a sled with a rope at
fifty newtons, thirty seven degrees above

110
00:06:39,461 --> 00:06:43,309
the ground. Find the horizontal and
vertical parts of the pull.

111
00:06:43,759 --> 00:06:44,954
Pause the video now.

112
00:06:45,404 --> 00:06:51,260
Horizontal: fifty times zero point eight,
forty newtons. Vertical: fifty times zero

113
00:06:51,260 --> 00:06:56,412
point six, thirty newtons. Only the forty
newtons pulls the sled forward.

114
00:06:56,712 --> 00:07:01,375
At the airport, you step onto a moving
walkway and keep walking. You zip past

115
00:07:01,375 --> 00:07:02,707
people standing still.

116
00:07:03,157 --> 00:07:05,674
Your speed depends on what you measure it
against.

117
00:07:06,824 --> 00:07:10,920
Every velocity is measured relative to
something, usually the ground.

118
00:07:11,370 --> 00:07:16,464
The walkway moves at one meter per second
relative to the ground. You walk at one

119
00:07:16,464 --> 00:07:19,797
point five meters per second relative to
the walkway.

120
00:07:20,947 --> 00:07:24,488
When the motions are in the same
direction, the velocities add.

121
00:07:24,938 --> 00:07:29,928
Relative to the ground, you move at one
plus one point five, which is two point

122
00:07:29,928 --> 00:07:31,381
five meters per second.

123
00:07:32,531 --> 00:07:35,197
Walk the wrong way on the walkway, and
they subtract.

124
00:07:35,647 --> 00:07:41,144
One point five minus one is only zero
point five meters per second relative to

125
00:07:41,144 --> 00:07:41,919
the ground.

126
00:07:43,069 --> 00:07:48,047
Boats work the same way. A boat goes six
meters per second in still water. The

127
00:07:48,047 --> 00:07:50,408
river flows at two meters per second.

128
00:07:50,858 --> 00:07:55,375
Heading downstream, the boat moves at six
plus two, which is eight meters per

129
00:07:55,375 --> 00:07:55,786
second.

130
00:07:56,236 --> 00:08:00,204
Heading upstream, it's six minus two, only
four meters per second.

131
00:08:01,354 --> 00:08:04,447
When the motions are at right angles, add
them as vectors.

132
00:08:04,897 --> 00:08:09,638
A boat heads straight across at six meters
per second, while the current pushes it

133
00:08:09,638 --> 00:08:10,679
downstream at two.

134
00:08:11,129 --> 00:08:15,826
Its speed relative to the shore is the
square root of six squared plus two

135
00:08:15,826 --> 00:08:18,937
squared, about six point three meters per
second.

136
00:08:20,087 --> 00:08:21,452
Now try one yourself.

137
00:08:21,902 --> 00:08:26,937
A plane flies at two hundred meters per
second relative to the air, straight into

138
00:08:26,937 --> 00:08:31,971
a headwind of thirty meters per second.
What is its speed relative to the ground?

139
00:08:32,421 --> 00:08:33,616
Pause the video now.

140
00:08:34,066 --> 00:08:39,182
The wind pushes against the plane, so
subtract. Two hundred minus thirty is one

141
00:08:39,182 --> 00:08:41,383
hundred seventy meters per second.

142
00:08:41,683 --> 00:08:44,840
Now let's work through a complete example,
step by step.

143
00:08:45,990 --> 00:08:50,962
A boat can move at four meters per second
relative to the water. It points straight

144
00:08:50,962 --> 00:08:53,478
across a river that is eighty meters wide.

145
00:08:53,928 --> 00:08:56,915
The river flows downstream at three meters
per second.

146
00:08:57,365 --> 00:09:01,773
Find the boat's speed relative to the
ground, the time to cross, and how far

147
00:09:01,773 --> 00:09:02,933
downstream it lands.

148
00:09:04,083 --> 00:09:09,091
The boat's velocity relative to the ground
is its velocity in the water, plus the

149
00:09:09,091 --> 00:09:10,142
water's velocity.

150
00:09:10,592 --> 00:09:15,407
Across and downstream are at right angles,
so they don't affect each other. The

151
00:09:15,407 --> 00:09:18,272
crossing time only depends on the across
speed.

152
00:09:19,422 --> 00:09:23,974
Ground speed: the square root of four
squared plus three squared, which is five

153
00:09:23,974 --> 00:09:25,011
meters per second.

154
00:09:25,461 --> 00:09:30,343
Crossing time: eighty meters divided by
four meters per second, which is twenty

155
00:09:30,343 --> 00:09:30,837
seconds.

156
00:09:31,287 --> 00:09:35,806
Drift: during those twenty seconds, the
current carries the boat three times

157
00:09:35,806 --> 00:09:37,708
twenty, sixty meters downstream.

158
00:09:38,858 --> 00:09:43,538
The boat crosses in twenty seconds, at
five meters per second over the ground,

159
00:09:43,538 --> 00:09:45,578
and lands sixty meters downstream.

160
00:09:46,028 --> 00:09:51,216
Tip: a common mistake is dividing the
width by five. The five meters per second

161
00:09:51,216 --> 00:09:53,580
is at an angle, not straight across.

162
00:09:54,030 --> 00:09:58,540
Tip: a faster current doesn't slow the
crossing. It only carries you farther

163
00:09:58,540 --> 00:09:59,193
downstream.

164
00:10:00,343 --> 00:10:05,487
Your turn. A boat moves at six meters per
second across a river one hundred twenty

165
00:10:05,487 --> 00:10:10,569
meters wide. The current is two meters per
second. Find the crossing time and the

166
00:10:10,569 --> 00:10:10,946
drift.

167
00:10:11,396 --> 00:10:12,590
Pause the video now.

168
00:10:13,040 --> 00:10:18,356
The time is one hundred twenty divided by
six, twenty seconds. The drift is two

169
00:10:18,356 --> 00:10:20,912
times twenty, forty meters downstream.

170
00:10:21,212 --> 00:10:25,329
A skateboarder rolls off the edge of a
ledge. How far out do they land?

171
00:10:25,779 --> 00:10:30,750
This is a horizontal projectile: something
launched sideways, then left to gravity.

172
00:10:31,900 --> 00:10:36,188
The trick is to treat the sideways motion
and the falling motion separately.

173
00:10:36,638 --> 00:10:41,950
Sideways, nothing pushes or pulls, so the
horizontal speed stays the same the whole

174
00:10:41,950 --> 00:10:42,270
time.

175
00:10:42,720 --> 00:10:46,688
Downward, the object falls exactly like
something dropped from rest.

176
00:10:47,838 --> 00:10:49,886
Look at the path in equal time steps.

177
00:10:50,336 --> 00:10:55,614
The sideways spacing stays the same. The
downward spacing grows, because the object

178
00:10:55,614 --> 00:10:57,013
speeds up as it falls.

179
00:10:57,463 --> 00:11:00,045
Together they make a curved path called a
parabola.

180
00:11:01,195 --> 00:11:02,197
Here's the method.

181
00:11:02,647 --> 00:11:07,730
Step one: use the height to find the time
of fall, from h equals one half g t

182
00:11:07,730 --> 00:11:08,258
squared.

183
00:11:08,708 --> 00:11:13,252
Step two: multiply the horizontal speed by
that time to get the distance.

184
00:11:14,402 --> 00:11:19,613
The skateboarder leaves a ledge one point
eight meters high at four meters per

185
00:11:19,613 --> 00:11:23,021
second. Use g equals ten meters per second
squared.

186
00:11:23,471 --> 00:11:29,545
One point eight equals five t squared, so
t squared is zero point three six, and t

187
00:11:29,545 --> 00:11:31,471
is zero point six seconds.

188
00:11:31,921 --> 00:11:37,467
The horizontal distance is four times zero
point six, which is two point four meters.

189
00:11:38,617 --> 00:11:40,132
Notice something surprising.

190
00:11:40,582 --> 00:11:45,427
Roll off faster, and you land farther
away. But the time in the air is the same,

191
00:11:45,427 --> 00:11:47,729
because it depends only on the height.

192
00:11:48,879 --> 00:11:50,244
Now try one yourself.

193
00:11:50,694 --> 00:11:55,292
A ball rolls off a table eighty
centimeters high, at two meters per

194
00:11:55,292 --> 00:11:59,547
second. Use g equals ten. How far from the
table does it land?

195
00:11:59,997 --> 00:12:01,192
Pause the video now.

196
00:12:01,642 --> 00:12:07,768
Zero point eight equals five t squared, so
t is zero point four seconds. The distance

197
00:12:07,768 --> 00:12:12,309
is two times zero point four, which is
zero point eight meters.

198
00:12:12,609 --> 00:12:15,766
Now let's work through a complete example,
step by step.

199
00:12:16,916 --> 00:12:22,146
A ball is thrown horizontally off a cliff
forty five meters high, at twelve meters

200
00:12:22,146 --> 00:12:22,847
per second.

201
00:12:23,297 --> 00:12:28,162
Use g equals ten. Find the time in the
air, how far out it lands, and its

202
00:12:28,162 --> 00:12:30,828
velocity just before it hits the ground.

203
00:12:31,978 --> 00:12:37,184
Treat horizontal and vertical separately.
Vertically, it starts from rest and falls

204
00:12:37,184 --> 00:12:38,314
forty five meters.

205
00:12:38,764 --> 00:12:42,966
Horizontally, the speed stays at twelve
meters per second the whole time.

206
00:12:43,416 --> 00:12:46,126
At the end, combine the two velocity
components.

207
00:12:47,276 --> 00:12:53,014
Time: forty five equals five t squared, so
t squared is nine, and t is three seconds.

208
00:12:53,464 --> 00:12:56,387
Distance: twelve times three is thirty six
meters.

209
00:12:56,837 --> 00:13:01,360
Final vertical speed: ten times three,
thirty meters per second downward.

210
00:13:01,810 --> 00:13:06,557
Impact speed: the square root of twelve
squared plus thirty squared, about thirty

211
00:13:06,557 --> 00:13:07,847
two meters per second.

212
00:13:08,297 --> 00:13:13,368
Angle: inverse tangent of thirty over
twelve, about sixty eight degrees below

213
00:13:13,368 --> 00:13:14,356
the horizontal.

214
00:13:15,506 --> 00:13:20,306
The ball is in the air three seconds,
lands thirty six meters out, and hits at

215
00:13:20,306 --> 00:13:22,460
about thirty two meters per second.

216
00:13:22,910 --> 00:13:27,721
Tip: the fall time depends only on the
height. Throwing harder doesn't keep it up

217
00:13:27,721 --> 00:13:28,137
longer.

218
00:13:28,587 --> 00:13:33,536
Tip: the impact speed is not just thirty.
Include the horizontal part too.

219
00:13:34,686 --> 00:13:39,860
Your turn. A plane flying level at forty
meters per second drops a package from one

220
00:13:39,860 --> 00:13:44,785
hundred twenty five meters up. How long
does it fall, and how far ahead does it

221
00:13:44,785 --> 00:13:45,097
land?

222
00:13:45,547 --> 00:13:46,742
Pause the video now.

223
00:13:47,192 --> 00:13:52,744
One twenty five equals five t squared, so
t is five seconds. It lands forty times

224
00:13:52,744 --> 00:13:56,514
five, two hundred meters ahead of where it
was dropped.

225
00:13:56,814 --> 00:14:01,742
A soccer ball kicked up at an angle rises,
slows, and comes back down in an arch.

226
00:14:02,192 --> 00:14:06,629
Let's find how long it stays up, how high
it goes, and how far it travels.

227
00:14:07,779 --> 00:14:10,894
Start by splitting the launch velocity
into components.

228
00:14:11,344 --> 00:14:16,144
The horizontal part, v cosine theta, stays
constant for the whole flight.

229
00:14:16,594 --> 00:14:20,669
The vertical part, v sine theta, is the
one gravity changes.

230
00:14:21,819 --> 00:14:27,304
Take a kick at twenty meters per second,
at thirty degrees. Use g equals ten meters

231
00:14:27,304 --> 00:14:28,560
per second squared.

232
00:14:29,010 --> 00:14:34,556
The horizontal component is twenty cosine
thirty, about seventeen point three meters

233
00:14:34,556 --> 00:14:35,282
per second.

234
00:14:35,732 --> 00:14:40,255
The vertical component is twenty sine
thirty, which is ten meters per second.

235
00:14:41,405 --> 00:14:45,885
Going up, the vertical speed drops by ten
meters per second every second.

236
00:14:46,335 --> 00:14:48,895
It reaches zero at the top after one
second.

237
00:14:49,345 --> 00:14:54,721
The maximum height is the average vertical
speed, five meters per second, times one

238
00:14:54,721 --> 00:14:56,470
second. That's five meters.

239
00:14:57,620 --> 00:15:01,988
The trip down takes as long as the trip
up, so the ball is in the air for two

240
00:15:01,988 --> 00:15:02,441
seconds.

241
00:15:02,891 --> 00:15:07,931
In that time, it moves seventeen point
three meters per second sideways. The

242
00:15:07,931 --> 00:15:10,849
range is about thirty four point six
meters.

243
00:15:11,999 --> 00:15:14,985
Which angle sends the ball farthest on
level ground?

244
00:15:15,435 --> 00:15:17,889
Forty five degrees gives the longest
range.

245
00:15:18,339 --> 00:15:23,276
And angles that add up to ninety degrees,
like thirty and sixty, give the same

246
00:15:23,276 --> 00:15:25,997
range. The higher one just stays up
longer.

247
00:15:27,147 --> 00:15:28,513
Now try one yourself.

248
00:15:28,963 --> 00:15:33,825
A ball is launched at ten meters per
second, at an angle where its vertical

249
00:15:33,825 --> 00:15:38,817
component is six and its horizontal
component is eight meters per second. Use

250
00:15:38,817 --> 00:15:42,253
g equals ten. Find its time in the air and
its range.

251
00:15:42,703 --> 00:15:43,898
Pause the video now.

252
00:15:44,348 --> 00:15:50,046
It takes six divided by ten, or zero point
six seconds, to reach the top, so it's in

253
00:15:50,046 --> 00:15:55,473
the air for one point two seconds. The
range is eight times one point two, which

254
00:15:55,473 --> 00:15:57,169
is nine point six meters.

255
00:15:57,470 --> 00:16:00,627
Now let's work through a complete example,
step by step.

256
00:16:01,777 --> 00:16:06,687
A soccer ball is kicked at twenty meters
per second, at thirty seven degrees above

257
00:16:06,687 --> 00:16:07,345
the ground.

258
00:16:07,795 --> 00:16:13,302
Use g equals ten, sine thirty seven is
zero point six, and cosine is zero point

259
00:16:13,302 --> 00:16:17,971
eight. Find the maximum height, the time
in the air, and the range.

260
00:16:19,121 --> 00:16:21,617
Split the launch velocity into components.

261
00:16:22,067 --> 00:16:26,355
At the top, the vertical speed is zero.
That gives the time up, and the height.

262
00:16:26,805 --> 00:16:32,540
On level ground, the time down equals the
time up. The range is the horizontal speed

263
00:16:32,540 --> 00:16:33,973
times the total time.

264
00:16:35,123 --> 00:16:40,577
Components: twenty times zero point eight
is sixteen meters per second across.

265
00:16:40,577 --> 00:16:44,702
Twenty times zero point six is twelve
meters per second up.

266
00:16:45,152 --> 00:16:49,163
Time to the top: twelve divided by ten,
one point two seconds.

267
00:16:49,613 --> 00:16:53,410
Height: twelve squared over twenty, which
is seven point two meters.

268
00:16:53,860 --> 00:16:57,807
Total time: twice one point two, which is
two point four seconds.

269
00:16:58,257 --> 00:17:02,374
Range: sixteen times two point four,
thirty eight point four meters.

270
00:17:03,524 --> 00:17:08,739
The ball rises seven point two meters,
stays up two point four seconds, and lands

271
00:17:08,739 --> 00:17:10,735
about thirty eight meters away.

272
00:17:11,185 --> 00:17:15,758
Tip: at the top, the vertical speed is
zero, but the ball is still moving

273
00:17:15,758 --> 00:17:18,139
sideways at sixteen meters per second.

274
00:17:18,589 --> 00:17:23,750
Tip: use the vertical part for height and
time, and the horizontal part for range.

275
00:17:23,750 --> 00:17:24,883
Don't mix them up.

276
00:17:25,333 --> 00:17:29,683
Tip: the shortcut of doubling the time up
only works when it lands at the same

277
00:17:29,683 --> 00:17:30,687
height it started.

278
00:17:31,837 --> 00:17:36,924
Your turn. A ball is launched at twenty
five meters per second, with a vertical

279
00:17:36,924 --> 00:17:41,946
component of twenty and a horizontal
component of fifteen. Find its max height

280
00:17:41,946 --> 00:17:42,589
and range.

281
00:17:43,039 --> 00:17:44,234
Pause the video now.

282
00:17:44,684 --> 00:17:50,029
The height is twenty squared over twenty,
which is twenty meters. The time up is two

283
00:17:50,029 --> 00:17:54,865
seconds, so the total time is four
seconds. The range is fifteen times four,

284
00:17:54,865 --> 00:17:55,692
sixty meters.

285
00:17:55,992 --> 00:18:00,729
Now let's put the whole unit together,
with one bigger problem that uses every

286
00:18:00,729 --> 00:18:01,944
idea from this unit.

287
00:18:03,094 --> 00:18:08,039
A cart rolls east at four meters per
second. A student on the cart throws a

288
00:18:08,039 --> 00:18:08,897
ball forward.

289
00:18:09,347 --> 00:18:13,994
Relative to the cart, the ball leaves at
ten meters per second, thirty seven

290
00:18:13,994 --> 00:18:15,768
degrees above the horizontal.

291
00:18:16,218 --> 00:18:22,052
Use g equals ten, sine thirty seven equals
zero point six, and cosine equals zero

292
00:18:22,052 --> 00:18:22,917
point eight.

293
00:18:23,367 --> 00:18:28,254
Find the ball's velocity relative to the
ground, how high it goes, and how far it

294
00:18:28,254 --> 00:18:29,703
travels over the ground.

295
00:18:30,853 --> 00:18:34,693
Lesson two: split the throw into
components, relative to the cart.

296
00:18:35,143 --> 00:18:40,624
Lesson three: add the cart's velocity to
get velocities relative to the ground. The

297
00:18:40,624 --> 00:18:44,124
cart only moves sideways, so only the x
part changes.

298
00:18:44,574 --> 00:18:48,627
Lesson one: combine the ground components
back into a single vector.

299
00:18:49,077 --> 00:18:52,341
Lessons four and five: then it's an
ordinary projectile.

300
00:18:53,491 --> 00:18:55,134
Part a, the launch velocity.

301
00:18:55,584 --> 00:19:01,240
Relative to the cart: ten times zero point
eight, eight meters per second forward.

302
00:19:01,240 --> 00:19:04,757
Ten times zero point six, six meters per
second up.

303
00:19:05,207 --> 00:19:09,970
Relative to the ground, add the cart's
four: twelve meters per second forward.

304
00:19:09,970 --> 00:19:11,863
The vertical part is still six.

305
00:19:12,313 --> 00:19:16,903
The size: the square root of twelve
squared plus six squared, about thirteen

306
00:19:16,903 --> 00:19:20,527
point four meters per second, at about
twenty seven degrees.

307
00:19:20,977 --> 00:19:22,299
Parts b, c and d.

308
00:19:22,749 --> 00:19:28,068
Time up: six over ten, zero point six
seconds. Total time: one point two

309
00:19:28,068 --> 00:19:28,659
seconds.

310
00:19:29,109 --> 00:19:32,543
Max height: six squared over twenty, one
point eight meters.

311
00:19:32,993 --> 00:19:37,367
Ground range: twelve times one point two,
fourteen point four meters.

312
00:19:37,817 --> 00:19:43,328
Meanwhile the cart moves four times one
point two, four point eight meters. So the

313
00:19:43,328 --> 00:19:46,755
ball lands nine point six meters ahead of
the cart.

314
00:19:47,905 --> 00:19:52,902
Over the ground, the ball leaves at about
thirteen meters per second, rises one

315
00:19:52,902 --> 00:19:56,823
point eight meters, and lands fourteen
point four meters away.

316
00:19:57,273 --> 00:20:02,968
Notice: nine point six meters is exactly
the range the student would see on a cart

317
00:20:02,968 --> 00:20:06,233
at rest. Relative to the cart, nothing
changes.

318
00:20:06,683 --> 00:20:11,742
Tip: the cart's motion is horizontal, so
it never changes the height or the time in

319
00:20:11,742 --> 00:20:12,229
the air.

320
00:20:12,679 --> 00:20:16,221
Tip: always write which frame each
velocity is measured in.

321
00:20:17,371 --> 00:20:22,279
Your turn. Same cart, same throw, but now
the student throws backward, toward the

322
00:20:22,279 --> 00:20:25,733
west. Where does the ball land, measured
from the ground?

323
00:20:26,183 --> 00:20:27,378
Pause the video now.

324
00:20:27,828 --> 00:20:33,111
Horizontally, eight west plus four east is
four meters per second west. The time is

325
00:20:33,111 --> 00:20:38,075
still one point two seconds, so the ball
lands four point eight meters west of

326
00:20:38,075 --> 00:20:39,348
where it was thrown.

327
00:20:39,798 --> 00:20:44,790
That completes Unit 2. Next, we start Unit
3: forces and Newton's laws.

328
00:20:45,940 --> 00:20:50,640
If this lesson helped, subscribe to School
STEM, Explained, and tap the bell so you

329
00:20:50,640 --> 00:20:51,999
don't miss the next one.

